export const clauses=[
['Purpose and consideration','This proposed agreement is between the named landlord and guarantor. In return for the landlord agreeing, in reliance on this guarantee, to grant the identified tenancy to the named applicants, the guarantor promises to meet only the obligations expressly defined below. The agreed tenancy and guarantee versions must be supplied before signing. This witness-free route is intended for a new tenancy before the landlord is already bound to grant it; an existing tenancy, retrospective guarantee or late signature must be referred for legal advice on consideration and execution.'],
['Tenancy and separate guarantee period','The proposed tenancy is an assured periodic tenancy in England from 1 November 2026, with rent payable monthly. It has no contractual fixed end date. This proposal limits the guarantee to obligations arising between 1 November 2026 and 31 October 2027, ending earlier where the provisions below apply. That is a limit on this guarantee only; it does not end the tenancy or require the tenant to leave. There is no automatic renewal of the guarantee.'],
['Precisely limited responsibility','The schedule identifies one guaranteed applicant, [GUARANTEED_APPLICANT], and the other joint applicant, [OTHER_APPLICANT]. For this illustration, the guaranteed rent contribution is 50% of the £1,000 monthly rent, limited to £500 per month. The guarantor does not guarantee the other applicant’s separate contribution. Credit all payments attributable to the guaranteed contribution; if attribution is unclear, allocate payments fairly and provide the calculation. This limit does not change the tenants’ own joint liabilities under their tenancy.'],
['Other loss and the total cap','The guarantee also covers proven, reasonable loss from damage or another lawful tenancy obligation attributable to the named guaranteed applicant, only to the extent the applicant is legally liable. Exclude fair wear and tear, improvements, landlord repair duties, losses caused by the landlord and amounts that are prohibited or unfair. The total aggregate liability for rent, other loss, interest and recoverable costs together cannot exceed £6,000 under this proposed schedule. No separate indemnity expands that cap or avoids statutory protections. These financial limits are a proposal for review, not a statutory formula.'],
['Payment requests and evidence','Before demanding payment, provide the guarantor with a dated statement of the default, relevant tenancy term, rent ledger or damage evidence, credits received and calculation within the agreed limits. Allow a reasonable opportunity to query the statement, obtain advice and discuss a repayment plan. Inform the guarantor promptly of arrears rather than allow avoidable losses to accumulate. Do not recover the same loss twice: deduct payments, deposit allocations, insurance recoveries and sums from other responsible parties as appropriate.'],
['Changes to rent or occupants','A rent increase or other change does not automatically increase this guarantor’s monetary limits or scope. Any increased commitment, replacement property, replacement tenant, new tenancy, renewal or extension requires a fresh, informed written agreement and a legally valid basis for it. No blanket advance consent to unknown variations is given. Relevant changes must be referred to the office for review and, where necessary, new documents and signatures; the guarantor retains any legal right to discharge arising from a variation.'],
['Ending liability and death protections','The guarantee stops covering new liabilities when its stated period ends, the tenancy legally ends, the named guaranteed applicant dies, or the landlord releases the guarantor in writing, whichever occurs first. Valid liabilities accrued before that point remain subject to the cap and all defences. Additionally, nothing makes the guarantor liable for rent excluded by Housing Act 1988 sections 16N and 16P: this includes the sole tenant’s death, the death of all joint tenants, and the applicable family-member rules in joint tenancies. Apply statutory apportionment for the rent period containing the death. The express named-applicant death limit above is an additional contractual protection.'],
['Guarantor death or insolvency','The proposal ends liability for new obligations from the guarantor’s death. Existing accrued claims remain subject to the cap, estate administration and insolvency law. Bankruptcy, loss of capacity or insolvency of any party must be referred for legal review; this agreement does not disapply statutory protections or create liability for another guarantor’s commitments.'],
['Withdrawal and expiry','Before the proposed tenancy is granted, the guarantor may withdraw in writing and the office must stop relying on the guarantee; the landlord must then decide whether to proceed without it. After grant, a request for release or replacement must be considered and any agreed release recorded in writing. Do not describe the guarantee as irrevocable in all circumstances. Notify the office before the limited guarantee expires so future arrangements can be considered lawfully without assuming automatic renewal.'],
['Costs and disputes','No guarantor application, administration or referencing fee is payable under this agreement. There is no automatic liability for all legal fees. Any interest or costs sought must be lawful, reasonable, supported and within the overall cap; court costs remain subject to the court’s powers. Preserve defences, set-off, complaint routes and access to the courts. Observe any applicable pre-action protocol and allow the required response time before proceedings. No term authorises eviction, entry, deduction or enforcement without the proper legal process.'],
['Notices and personal information','The final schedule must identify the landlord’s address for notices and the agent’s authority to act. Provide reliable contact details for the guarantor and retain evidence of notices actually given; do not deem every email or posted item conclusively received. Privacy information must identify the controller, purposes, lawful bases, recipients, retention rules and rights. Identity evidence and bank statements are for authorised reviewers and referencing providers where necessary, not other applicants. A credit-check authority is separate from the financial guarantee and is not a waiver of data rights.'],
['Signing and legal rights','Use a signed written agreement with evidence of identity, intention, document version and date. The guarantor must have time to read the full proposed tenancy and guarantee and seek independent advice. A typed name in this demonstration has no legal effect. The final witness-free agreement must not say it is executed as a deed. If a deed is selected on legal advice, its execution and witnessing requirements must be restored. English law applies, subject to mandatory rights and the court’s jurisdiction; no term removes consumer or statutory protections.']];
